**Weaknesses:**

1. **Significant gap between theoretical assumptions and practical evaluation:** The theory assumes modular latent factorization (Eq. 2), per-module TV error bounds, calibrated uncertainty scores, and i.i.d. sampling. The synthetic experiments use independent AR(1) modules that trivially satisfy all assumptions, while the real-world experiment (Uganda LSMS) has no verification that the latent dynamics actually factorize modularity. This mirrors concerns raised about the modularity paper (unE3TZSAVZ): *"The scaling behaviour is studied theoretically in the case of linear neural networks for tractability. A more thorough empirical investigation to what extent this scaling law is practically relevant in the nonlinear setting would have been useful"* and *"Validation of theoretical results is only shown in the appendix, with large discrepancy between theoretical predictions and numerics."* Similarly, reviewers of the synthetic data theory paper (I9Dsq0cVo9) noted: *"The theoretical predictions are based on binary Gaussian mixture data... it is unclear how well these predictions, derived from Gaussian mixture models, will generalize to real-world data."*

2. **Limited experimental evaluation and baselines:** The paper evaluates on only one real-world dataset (Uganda LSMS household electrification), and the baselines are relatively minimal (factual-only models, self-supervised autoencoder, and pool-based active learning). No comparison is made with other data augmentation methods (such as SMOTE variants, generative augmentation, or even simple random augmentation), nor with other calibration-aware methods. This mirrors concerns from the counterfactual augmentation paper (AMCaG2TAeg): *"The novelty is limited... The conceptual leap from the work of CoDA... to the present technique of swapping uncontrollable subgraphs, seems incremental rather than revolutionary"* and *"empirical comparisons seem to lack a critical control condition."* Similarly, reviewers of the ν-ensembles paper (wHgu98u8Sc) criticized: *"experiments are conducted on small slices of CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100"* and *"lack the comparison with SOTA methods."*

3. **The modular factorization assumption is strong and largely unverified:** The assumption that latent dynamics factorize into modules with small parent sets (Eq. 2) is central to all theoretical results. The real-world dataset involves household covariates (energy spending, region, urban/rural) — it is unclear whether these naturally decompose into independent modules, and no verification (e.g., conditional independence tests, disentanglement metrics) is provided. This echoes criticisms of modular causal generation papers: *"in all the experiments presented in the paper, the underlying causal graph fortuitously satisfies the condition wherein the solitary high-dimensional nodes are isolated c-components. However, in more general cases... the method would still need to match [complex joint distributions], which does not significantly simplify the original problem"* (vbebD7QRxP). Relatedly, the world models paper (k7nYm2yU5i) was criticized for *"the assumption of requiring C3 functions with bounded Lipschitz derivatives for theoretical analysis can be very strong and potentially unrealistic in practice."*

4. **The calibrated acceptance guarantees rely on Assumption 3.6 that is hard to verify in practice:** The pointwise calibration assumption (U upper-bounds a per-sample discrepancy d) is critical for Theorem 3.8 and all downstream results, yet it is unclear how one would verify this holds in any real application. The paper uses ensemble variance as U but does not verify that it upper-bounds the discrepancy between P and Q. This mirrors reviewer concerns about unverified calibration assumptions in the AUGCAL paper (WNQjN5HzXt): *"It would be more interesting to provide more insights regarding this bound. For example, what issues (e.g., a large gap between sim and real) and why will those issues lead to the looseness of the upper bound?"*

5. **Unclear practical gains and lack of ablation studies:** The improvement from Ridge 0.227→0.219 in Table 2 (synthetic) is modest, and while the AUC improvement on LSMS (0.8797→0.9402 at n=25) is substantial, it is unclear how much of this gain comes from (a) modular recombination, (b) the uncertainty filter, versus (c) simply augmenting with more data from any source. No ablation isolating these components is presented. This echoes concerns from the counterfactual data augmentation paper (7mR83Q12cJ): *"missing... a comparison with an evident baseline: initializing the neural net architecture of the corresponding image node using a pre-trained marginal distribution"* and the generalization bounds paper (NkmJotfL42): *"which bounds in the existing literature are really 'in the overparametrized setting'?"* — i.e., is the bound the source of improvement, or is it just having more data?

6. **Presentation density and notation overload:** The paper packs a large number of theoretical results (Theorems 3.1, 3.5, 3.8, 3.10, 3.12, Corollaries 3.9, 3.11, 3.13, plus lemmas) with substantial notation into limited space, making it hard to parse the key insights. This matches concerns from the world models paper (k7nYm2yU5i): *"The presentation is quite dense and difficult to follow, with repetitive statements and inadequate motivation"* and *"the paper is written in a way that makes it unnecessarily difficult to understand."*

