### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

### 1. Claimed “train–val gap elimination” from the new split is weakly supported  
**Type: Evidential**  
**Sections: 3.2, Table 1, 4.1**

The paper’s first major claimed contribution is that their new three-way split (map-train / motion-train / motion-val) “eliminates the train-val gap” and is crucial for a fair protocol. The evidence is thin and somewhat contradictory:

- Table 1 compares four split settings but **does not actually demonstrate a problematic train–val gap in the prior protocol**. In the “default” split (row 3), the map model is trained on nuScenes train, the motion model is also trained on nuScenes train (with predicted maps from the same set), and evaluated on nuScenes val. That is conceptually similar to their proposed split in that **both training and validation use maps on unseen scenes** (validation scenes are different from training scenes, even if there is spatial overlap).
- The authors’ main complaint about the default protocol is that the online map model’s inference on its training set (for motion-train) leads to overly clean maps, unlike validation. But in Table 1 they compare:
  - (1) Their three-way, spatially disjoint split  
  - (2) Training both map and motion on map-train+motion-train  
  - (3) Default Gu et al. setting (nuScenes train/val)  
  - (4) A “two half-splits” variant on nuScenes train  
  The performance differences (e.g., minADE 0.6308 vs 0.6839 vs 0.6373) are **moderate and noisy**, and (4) — which still uses training-set inference to create motion inputs — yields performance almost identical to (1).
- The only thing directly linking these differences to a “train-val gap” is a narrative explanation. There is **no quantitative evidence** of:  
  - Map accuracy on train vs validation for the Gu et al. protocol,  
  - Motion-prediction performance when the motion model is evaluated on maps with systematically varied quality, or  
  - Overfitting behavior (e.g., much better performance when evaluating motion on maps generated for the map model’s training scenes).

As a result, the central narrative “default split produces severe train–val gap; our split eliminates it” is not established. The new split may well be more principled for other reasons (e.g., spatial disjointness for measuring map generalization), but the paper’s most prominent motivation is not backed by convincing empirical diagnosis. This undermines the strength of the benchmark-justification claim; the data could equally be interpreted as modest hyperparameter/tuning noise between four different ways of picking train/val scene sets.

To bring this to an acceptable evidential level, the authors would need explicit, controlled comparisons separating: (i) spatial overlap vs no-overlap in map training vs motion training vs validation, and (ii) training with maps produced on the map model’s training scenes vs unseen scenes, along with clear measures of map accuracy in each regime. In its current form, the argument is suggestive but not demonstrative.

---

### 2. “Boundary-free” image-feature baseline is underspecified and attribution of gains is ambiguous  
**Type: Evidential**  
**Sections: 3.3 (boundary-free baseline), Fig. 7, Eq. (1), Table 4 (implicit), Table 7**

The paper’s headline technical contribution beyond the benchmark is the “boundary-free baseline” that uses deformable attention from each agent to raw image features to handle out-of-range agents. There are several issues that weaken this claim:

- **Architectural details are extremely sparse.** Section 3.3 gives only:
  - A generic deformable attention formula \( \hat{A}_i = \text{DeformAtt}(A_i, p_i, I_{T(i)}) \)
  - A text description that each agent is projected into camera views and aggregates features.
  There is no description of:
  - How many sampling points and heads, what resolution levels, or which image backbone are used.
  - Where exactly this module is inserted in HiVT or DenseTNT (pre-encoder, fused with agent embeddings, separate branch, etc.).
  - How BEV map information and image features are combined (additive, concatenation with MLP, attention fusion, etc.).
  For a benchmark paper, this is not fatal for reproducibility only because they promise code, but **it is fatal for evaluating the claimed causal mechanism** (“mitigating the unaligned range issue”) — we cannot tell what else changed in the network.
- **No ablation for in-range vs out-of-range agents.** The central narrative is: maps have limited spatial coverage; image features do not; therefore image fusion especially helps far agents. Table 7 shows that the “img” variant improves minADE for both close and far agents, but:
  - There is no ablation that applies the same fusion only to in-range agents vs out-of-range ones.
  - There is no comparison where the same deformable-attention module is applied to BEV features only within the map range, to isolate “boundary-free” as the key difference rather than just “more features / more capacity”.
  - There is no evaluation of whether far agents actually lie outside the map range for each case; “far” is defined by distance relative to the **nominal perception range**, but the map model might still produce some partial geometry.
- **No fairness check with respect to prior methods (unc, bev).** The “unc” and “bev” baselines from Gu et al. (2024a,b) are compared to “img”, but:
  - We are not told whether “unc” and “bev” are re-implemented under the authors’ modified training protocol, and with the same motion models, or directly copied and partially adapted.
  - There is no evidence that hyperparameters (e.g., number of training steps, data augmentations) are matched across base / unc / bev / img. The improvements (often at the few-percent level) could plausibly be accounted for by general capacity or training tweaks rather than the specific out-of-range argument.

Because of this, the paper **does not yet establish** that the proposed image-feature fusion is specifically solving the “perception-range mismatch” problem, as opposed to simply giving the motion model a more powerful sensory pathway. The claims that it “addresses the issue of agents extending beyond the map’s perceptual boundaries” and “achieves SOTA performance” are overstated relative to the experimental isolation.

